secur32: answer GetUserNameExW's NameUserPrincipal and NameDnsDomain from the machine's domain

`GetUserNameExW` implements only `NameSamCompatible`; every other format falls through to the FIXME arm and
returns `ERROR_NONE_MAPPED` (1332).  Format 8 - `NameUserPrincipal` - is the one Power BI Desktop's identity
path calls: `PBI.PQ.AcquirePlatformEmail` -> `SignInService.SignInInteractive` ->
`AuthenticationManager.GetPlatformEmail` -> `PlatformEmailProvider.TryGetPlatformEmail` ->
`Secur32Wrapper.GetUserName` -> `[DllImport("secur32.dll")] GetUserNameEx(ExtendedNameFormat.NameUserPrincipal,
...)` (decompiled in `docs/DATA_FETCH_ERROR.md` §12.1, which also records the two-call protocol: a NULL-buffer
size probe expected to answer FALSE + `ERROR_MORE_DATA` (234), then the call with a buffer of that size).
Under Wine the *sizing* call fails, the provider returns false without ever asking again, and the
application's own trace records it (`expectedErrorCode` is still -1, i.e. the first call is the one that
failed):

    {"Action":"PBI.PQ.AcquirePlatformEmail","foundUserEmail":"False","expectedErrorCode":"-1",
     "errorCode":"1332",...,"isError":"True"}

Windows answers these two formats from the account's directory entry - "user@dns.domain" for
`NameUserPrincipal`, "dns.domain\user" for `NameDnsDomain` - and answers `ERROR_NONE_MAPPED` when the account
has no such entry.  The account's `userPrincipalName` lives in AD and Wine has no directory, so the domain is
taken from the machine's DNS domain: that is exactly the value Wine's LSA already reports as
`POLICY_DNS_DOMAIN_INFO.DnsDomainName` (`advapi32/lsa.c` builds it from
`GetComputerNameExW(ComputerNameDnsDomain)`, and `GetComputerObjectNameW` in this same file consults that
policy too).  When the machine is in no domain the function keeps returning `ERROR_NONE_MAPPED`, which is
what Windows does in that case: "If the user account is not in a domain, only NameSamCompatible is
supported" (GetUserNameEx documentation) - measured on the Windows 11 guest, where formats 3, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10
and 12 all answer `ERROR_NONE_MAPPED` for the local account and format 2 answers normally.  The two new cases
therefore only fire where Windows would have an answer, and the buffer contract is the same as the existing
`NameSamCompatible` case (success: `*nSize` = length without the terminating null; too small:
`ERROR_MORE_DATA` with `*nSize` = length + 1 and the buffer untouched).

This is the `secur32` change the AutoCAD 2027 on Wine project's series carries as its patch `0014`
(`cad-wine-main/patches/0014-secur32-GetUserNameExW-NameUserPrincipal-NameDnsDomain.patch`), ported here
rather than reinvented: the hunk below is identical to that one.  It has to be, because the fork in this repo
is built on that project's tree, so `dlls/secur32/secur32.c` already differed from pristine by exactly this
change while `patches/0001..0004` did not carry it - the series only *excused* the file through
`check_patch_series.sh`'s `BASELINE_FILES` list.  With `0005` the series is self-contained for it (the file is
now patch-touched, so the hard byte-identity check applies to it instead).

Verified with `tools/winapi/nameprobe.c` - mingw, one binary for both platforms, everything resolved through
`LoadLibrary`/`GetProcAddress` so the answering module is visible in the output.  It prints, for formats 2, 3,
6, 7, 8, 9, 10 and 12, the raw return value plus `GetLastError()` and the in/out size of the three calls the
contract has: the NULL-buffer sizing call the application makes, the sized call, and a deliberately too-small
buffer.  It also prints the registry value and the `GetComputerNameExW` the new answers are built from.
`tools/winapi/run_nameprobe.sh` reproduces the Wine captures into `logs/secur32/`.

Same machine, same probe; the only difference is whether the machine has a DNS domain, and the Windows guest
is the reference for the state it is actually in:

  * No DNS domain - the state every prefix on this host is in, because wineboot's
    `create_computer_name_keys()` syncs `Tcpip\Parameters\Domain` from the host's canonical name, which
    carries no domain suffix here.  Pristine `secur32`, this patch, and the Windows guest all answer the
    same thing, so the patch invents nothing that Windows withholds:

        Wine      logs/secur32/nameprobe_wine_before_nodomain.txt  (pristine secur32)
        Wine      logs/secur32/nameprobe_wine_after_nodomain.txt   (this patch)
        Windows   logs/secur32/nameprobe_win_workgroup.txt         (guest: WORKGROUP, no DNS suffix)

              GetComputerNameExW(2 DnsDomain)         ret=1 len=0 name=""
              GetUserNameExW( 8 NameUserPrincipal) sizing NULL ret=0 lastError=1332 nSize=0
              GetUserNameExW(12 NameDnsDomain)     sizing NULL ret=0 lastError=1332 nSize=0
              GetUserNameExW( 2 NameSamCompatible) sizing NULL ret=0 lastError=234  nSize=10  (Windows)
                                                             ... ret=1 lastError=0    name="asdf\adsf"

    (The guest prints a stale `lastError=203` next to `GetComputerNameExW`'s success - Windows does not clear
    the last error on success; the return value and length there are the datum.)

  * With `HKLM\System\CurrentControlSet\Services\Tcpip\Parameters\Domain = corp.example.com` -
    `logs/secur32/nameprobe_wine_before_domain.txt` and `..._after_domain.txt`.  The value has to be set in
    the same Wine session as the probe because wineboot resets it on the next launch; both runs print
    `GetComputerNameExW(2 DnsDomain) -> "corp.example.com"`, i.e. the domain *is* available in both, and only
    the `secur32` under test differs:

        pristine secur32 (before)                        this patch (after)
        NameUserPrincipal sizing    FALSE err=1332 nSize=0     FALSE err=234  nSize=22
        NameUserPrincipal call      FALSE err=1332             TRUE  err=0    nSize=21 "asdf@corp.example.com"
        NameDnsDomain     sizing    FALSE err=1332 nSize=0     FALSE err=234  nSize=22
        NameDnsDomain     call      FALSE err=1332             TRUE  err=0    nSize=21 "corp.example.com\asdf"
        NameUserPrincipal, 1-WCHAR buffer                      FALSE err=234  nSize=22, buffer untouched
        NameSamCompatible (either)  unchanged: 234/nSize=21, then TRUE nSize=20 "ASDF-THINKPAD-E\asdf"

    The domain-configured *Windows* reference could not be taken, and it is the one measurement missing here:
    the guest's command channel runs non-elevated (measured: `IsInRole(Administrator)=False`) so the temporary
    `Tcpip\Parameters\Domain` write is refused - `Set-ItemProperty` reports "Requested registry access is not
    allowed" and the value reads back empty - and raising UAC on the shared desktop (the guest is in use for
    another measurement) is not acceptable.  The guest's native state is therefore the Windows side of this
    patch's differential, and the domain-configured answer is Windows' documented one, which is what the two
    new cases implement.

Scope, stated plainly.  This closes the identity gap the in-tree AutoCAD series was carrying by accident; it
does **not** make the report canvas render - FINDINGS M50 measures that failure to the provider's *chaptered*
row fetch (`IRowset::GetNextRows` -> `DB_E_ERRORSINCOMMAND`), which happens whether or not the user is signed
in.  It also does not change what the application sees on this host's prefixes: with no DNS domain configured,
patched Wine answers `1332` exactly like Windows on this workgroup machine, so the `PBI.PQ.AcquirePlatformEmail`
trace record is unchanged here and only a domain-configured machine gets the principal name.  Formats 3, 6, 7,
9 and 10 still answer `ERROR_NONE_MAPPED`, as Windows does for a local account; `NameUserPrincipal` is composed
from the machine's DNS domain and the local account name, not from an AD `userPrincipalName` attribute, so it
is the right *shape* rather than a directory lookup.  It is a candidate explanation for FINDINGS M44's finding
that the AutoCAD series is load-bearing for A3 (same gap, same file, the API behind `AcquirePlatformEmail`),
and with `0005` in the series that can now be tested as pristine + `0001..0005` instead of pristine + CAD.
--- a/dlls/secur32/secur32.c
+++ b/dlls/secur32/secur32.c
@@ -1119,6 +1119,22 @@
     return rc;
 }
 
+/* The DNS domain the machine is joined to, i.e. the domain the current account's user principal name
+ * is formed with.  Windows takes it from the account's userPrincipalName attribute in AD; Wine has no
+ * AD, so use the machine's DNS domain - the very same value Wine's LSA reports as
+ * POLICY_DNS_DOMAIN_INFO.DnsDomainName (advapi32/lsa.c builds it from
+ * GetComputerNameExW(ComputerNameDnsDomain), and GetComputerObjectNameW above uses that policy too).
+ * Returns FALSE when the machine is not in a domain, which is also the condition Windows uses:
+ * "If the user account is not in a domain, only NameSamCompatible is supported" (GetUserNameEx
+ * documentation), i.e. the other formats answer ERROR_NONE_MAPPED there. */
+static BOOL get_dns_domain( WCHAR *domain, DWORD len )
+{
+    DWORD size = len;
+
+    if (!GetComputerNameExW( ComputerNameDnsDomain, domain, &size )) return FALSE;
+    return size != 0 && domain[0] != 0;
+}
+
 BOOLEAN WINAPI GetUserNameExW(
   EXTENDED_NAME_FORMAT NameFormat, LPWSTR lpNameBuffer, PULONG nSize)
 {
@@ -1155,15 +1171,52 @@
             return FALSE;
         }
 
+    case NameUserPrincipal:
+    case NameDnsDomain:
+        {
+            WCHAR user[UNLEN + 1], domain[256];
+            DWORD user_len = ARRAY_SIZE(user), len;
+
+            if (!GetUserNameW(user, &user_len)) return FALSE;
+
+            if (!get_dns_domain(domain, ARRAY_SIZE(domain)))
+            {
+                SetLastError(ERROR_NONE_MAPPED);
+                return FALSE;
+            }
+
+            /* NameUserPrincipal is "user@dns.domain", NameDnsDomain is "dns.domain\user" */
+            len = lstrlenW(user) + lstrlenW(domain) + 1;
+            if (lpNameBuffer && *nSize > len)
+            {
+                if (NameFormat == NameUserPrincipal)
+                {
+                    lstrcpyW(lpNameBuffer, user);
+                    lstrcatW(lpNameBuffer, L"@");
+                    lstrcatW(lpNameBuffer, domain);
+                }
+                else
+                {
+                    lstrcpyW(lpNameBuffer, domain);
+                    lstrcatW(lpNameBuffer, L"\\");
+                    lstrcatW(lpNameBuffer, user);
+                }
+                *nSize = len;
+                return TRUE;
+            }
+
+            SetLastError(ERROR_MORE_DATA);
+            *nSize = len + 1;
+            return FALSE;
+        }
+
     case NameUnknown:
     case NameFullyQualifiedDN:
     case NameDisplay:
     case NameUniqueId:
     case NameCanonical:
-    case NameUserPrincipal:
     case NameCanonicalEx:
     case NameServicePrincipal:
-    case NameDnsDomain:
         FIXME("NameFormat %d not implemented\n", NameFormat);
         SetLastError(ERROR_NONE_MAPPED);
         return FALSE;
